        "summary":  "Cohere released North Small Translate, an open-weight machine translation model supporting 50 languages that outperforms Google Translate and DeepL on WMT26 benchmarks. The 218-billion-parameter mixture-of-experts model deliberately uses non-reasoning pattern-matching for efficiency and is designed to run within an organization\u0027s own infrastructure for data sovereignty. An agentic multi-pass version can review and correct its own output, with support for custom terminology guides.",
        "execSummary":  "Cohere is positioning data sovereignty — not raw model quality — as the enterprise differentiator in AI translation, shipping a model that runs entirely inside a customer\u0027s own infrastructure so sensitive documents never leave the organization. For global enterprises processing legal contracts, support tickets, or regulated communications in non-English languages, this is a direct answer to the compliance and privacy concerns that have blocked cloud-based translation adoption. The benchmark performance against Google Translate and DeepL is compelling, but enterprise buyers should pressure-test the 50-language claim against their actual language mix, since \u0027supported\u0027 and \u0027production-quality\u0027 often diverge. The non-reasoning architecture is a deliberate trade-off — faster and cheaper to run on-premise, but may underperform reasoning models on ambiguous or domain-specific texts.",
