        "source":  "Microsoft AI Blog",
        "url":  "https://news.microsoft.com/signal/articles/how-to-build-an-ai-agent-guide/",
        "date":  "2026-08-10",
        "theme":  "Agents",
        "summary":  "Microsoft published a five-step no-code framework for building AI agents using Microsoft Copilot: start with the business problem, check the prebuilt agent store before building custom, use natural language to generate an agent draft, connect it to knowledge sources like SharePoint and email, then test and scale. The guide distinguishes agents from chatbots by their ability to take action — automating workflows like security incident response, inbox management, and report generation. The approach is positioned as accessible to workers in all roles without requiring coding expertise.",
        "execSummary":  "Microsoft is deliberately lowering the barrier to AI agent creation to accelerate Copilot adoption across non-technical enterprise workers, which has significant implications for how quickly organizations will see agent proliferation inside their Microsoft 365 environments. For customer-support and operations leaders, the emphasis on agents as workflow-action tools — not just Q\u0026A chatbots — maps directly to use cases like ticket routing, escalation management, and status report automation. The guide\u0027s \u0027check the prebuilt store first\u0027 step reveals Microsoft\u0027s strategy: standardize on a catalog of enterprise-grade agents before organizations build fragmented custom solutions. The caveat is that accessibility-first design often trades depth for breadth — leaders should validate that Copilot-built agents meet their governance, security, and integration requirements before broad deployment.",
