        "summary":  "AWS published a technical guide for building a contract intelligence platform using Amazon Quick and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore that overcomes the fundamental RAG limitation of single-document retrieval, enabling portfolio-wide aggregation across hundreds of contracts. The architecture uses dual AI agents — Claude Sonnet for extraction and Claude Haiku for independent validation — with Amazon Textract as a deterministic tiebreaker for ambiguous cases. Structured data flows into Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL enabling natural language querying, at a demonstrated inference cost of $0.014 per contract.",
        "execSummary":  "This reference architecture tackles one of the most pressing enterprise AI gaps: not just finding information in a document, but answering aggregated questions across an entire contract portfolio — a task that currently consumes weeks of analyst time. The multi-agent verification pattern (extraction agent + validation agent + deterministic tiebreaker) is a practical blueprint for any enterprise deploying AI on high-stakes documents where accuracy errors carry legal or financial consequences. At $0.014 per contract, the serverless economics make large-scale deployment viable even for mid-market firms. Key caveat: model performance must be validated against your specific contract formats before production deployment — generic benchmarks do not substitute for domain-specific testing.",
